
====================================================================================================
S1. FAMILY 4a -- vary the acceleration-smeared action
====================================================================================================
  S = -INT dt m a0^2 Psi(M/a0^2),   M(t) = INT w(t-t') |xddot(t')|^2 dt'
  delta M(t) = INT dt' w(t-t') 2 xddot(t').delta xddot(t'), so after swapping the order and
  defining the SMEARED response  P(t') = INT dt Psi'(M(t)/a0^2) w(t-t'),
      delta S = -2m INT dt' P(t') xddot(t').delta xddot(t')
  and integrating by parts TWICE to free delta x:
      EOM:   2 d^2/dt^2 [ P(t) xddot(t) ]  =  -grad Phi
  That is FOURTH order in x. Condition (ii) already fails. But check (i) too -- the circular
  reduction, where P is constant because |xddot| is constant:
  2 d^2/dt^2[P0 xddot] = Matrix([[2*A*Omega**2*P_0*cos(Omega*t), 2*A*Omega**2*P_0*sin(Omega*t)]])  (i.e. 2 P0 A Omega^2 outward)
  radial coefficient / (2 P0 A) = Omega**2
  [OK] the circular reduction of 4a is 2 P0 A Omega^2 = g_bar -- it carries an explicit Omega^2
  Now use the circular relation A = Omega^2 R to eliminate Omega:
      2 P0 A * (A/R) = g_bar     =>     2 P0 A^2 / R = g_bar
  The framework's law is  mu_fw(A/a0) A = g_bar. Equating:
      2 P0 A / R = mu_fw(A/a0)
  P0 = Psi'(A^2/a0^2) depends ONLY on A. The left side carries an explicit 1/R; the right
  side has none. So the two agree only for one radius per acceleration -- i.e. for a single
  potential, not as a law.
  [OK] FAMILY 4a FAILS BOTH CONDITIONS: fourth-order EOM (ii), and a circular reduction with a spurious 1/R that the algebraic law does not have (i)
  DIAGNOSIS. 4a has no ordinary kinetic term, so it has no Newtonian limit to begin with --
  that missing piece is exactly what the stray Omega^2 is reporting. The nonlocality has to
  live in the VELOCITY sector, not purely in the acceleration. That is sub-form 4b.

====================================================================================================
S2. FAMILY 4b -- Milgrom's nonlocal-MI class. Condition (ii) is MET; (i) is not.
====================================================================================================
  S = INT dt INT dt' Q(t-t') xdot(t).xdot(t')  -  INT dt m Phi(x)
  delta S = 2 INT dt INT dt' Q(t-t') xdot(t').delta xdot(t) - INT dt m grad Phi . delta x
  One integration by parts (only ONE, because the action is quadratic in VELOCITY):
      EOM:   2 d/dt [ INT dt' Q(t-t') xdot(t') ]  =  -m grad Phi
  => SECOND ORDER. Condition (ii) IS SATISFIED. This is why Milgrom's class is the right
  place to look, and it is a genuine point in Family 4's favour.

  Circular reduction: xdot rotates at Omega, so the convolution is diagonal --
      INT dt' Q(t-t') xdot(t') = Qtilde(Omega) xdot(t),   Qtilde = Fourier transform of Q
  hence  2 Qtilde(Omega) xddot = -m grad Phi, i.e.  2 Qtilde(Omega) A = g_bar.
  Comparing with the law mu_fw(A/a0) A = g_bar requires
      2 Qtilde(Omega) = mu_fw(A/a0) .
  THE PROBLEM: Qtilde depends on FREQUENCY only; mu_fw depends on ACCELERATION. On a circular
  orbit A = Omega^2 R, so at FIXED Omega the required mu_fw still varies with R. A fixed
  kernel cannot supply two different values at the same frequency. Quantified:
   Omega (1/s)   R (kpc)   A = Om^2 R      A/a0  required 2Qtilde
      1.00e-16       2.0    6.171e-13    0.0066           0.00659
      1.00e-16      10.0    3.086e-12    0.0330           0.03293
      1.00e-16      40.0    1.234e-11    0.1319           0.12965
      at this single Omega the required 2Qtilde spans 0.00659 to 0.12965  -- ratio 19.66x
      3.00e-16       2.0    5.554e-12    0.0593           0.05913
      3.00e-16      10.0    2.777e-11    0.2967           0.27437
      3.00e-16      40.0    1.111e-10    1.1868           0.66382
      at this single Omega the required 2Qtilde spans 0.05913 to 0.66382  -- ratio 11.23x
  [OK] at FIXED Omega the required kernel value varies by up to 19.66x across radii, so no frequency-only Qtilde can reproduce the law -- CONDITION (i) FAILS for 4b
  This is the SAME obstruction as Theorem 8, now seen from the variational side: a kernel
  diagonal in frequency cannot deliver a response that depends on acceleration.

====================================================================================================
S3. THE GENERAL OBSTRUCTION -- stated as sharply as the computation supports
====================================================================================================
  Any action built from a FIXED (potential-independent) kernel is, by translation invariance
  in time, DIAGONAL IN FREQUENCY. The framework's law is DIAGONAL IN ACCELERATION. On circular
  orbits the two labels are related by A = Omega^2 R, so a single frequency corresponds to a
  one-parameter family of accelerations as R varies -- and the law demands a different
  response for each. Therefore:

      NO ACTION WITH A FIXED KERNEL -- local or nonlocal, in velocity or in acceleration --
      REPRODUCES mu_fw(|a|/a0) a = g_bar FOR ALL POTENTIALS.

  The three earlier families were three ways of hitting this same wall; Family 4 makes the
  wall visible because 4b satisfies every OTHER requirement and still fails.
  [OK] the obstruction is stated with its scope: fixed-kernel actions, all potentials
  FOOTING-INDEPENDENT: the argument is about the LABELS (frequency vs acceleration), not
  about a0's value. Both footings give the same conclusion by inspection.

====================================================================================================
S4. CREDIT, and what status the law actually has
====================================================================================================
  MILGROM 1994 (Annals of Physics 229, 384) constructed nonlocal modified-inertia theories in
  exactly class 4b and showed they can carry the correct MOND and Newtonian LIMITS while
  preserving Galilean invariance. Nothing here contradicts that, and the credit is his.
  What S2-S3 add is narrower and sharper: such theories deliver the LIMITS but not the exact
  interpolating law, because the limits are asymptotic statements while mu_fw(|a|/a0) is a
  pointwise algebraic relation, and the pointwise relation is what the RAR fits use.

  SO THE HONEST STATUS OF THE FRAMEWORK'S LAW:
   * It is a PHENOMENOLOGICAL INTERPOLATION, fitted and remarkably successful (0.108 dex on
     SPARC, beating regular MOND's 0.122-0.140 on the framework's own footing).
   * It is EXACT as a first spectral moment (Theorem 1) -- that result stands untouched.
   * It is NOT the Euler-Lagrange equation of any fixed-kernel action, and now four families
     have been checked rather than assumed.
   * The correct claim is therefore 'a law with an action-based motivation and an exact
     moment interpretation', NOT 'a law derived from an action'. Those are different and the
     corpus should say the first.
  [OK] the law's status is restated at the level the computation supports

====================================================================================================
S5. WHERE THE VARIATIONAL ROUTE ACTUALLY LEADS -- and it leads out of modified inertia
====================================================================================================
  S3's obstruction assumed the kernel is POTENTIAL-INDEPENDENT. The one way to evade it is a
  kernel that KNOWS about the local field -- i.e. one sourced by the matter distribution.
  But a field-sourced, potential-dependent response is precisely a modified-GRAVITY theory:
  the extra structure lives in a field equation, not in the particle's inertia. AQUAL and
  QUMOND are exactly that, and they DO have variational principles that reproduce
  MOND-like laws.

  So the conclusion is structural and worth stating plainly: MAKING THE FRAMEWORK'S LAW
  VARIATIONAL PUSHES IT TOWARD MODIFIED GRAVITY. That is not a defect of the calculation --
  it is why the corpus keeps finding MI == AeST(=MG) to machine precision in static systems,
  and it is consistent with the published lensing construction being disformal (a metric
  statement) rather than an inertia statement.

  WHAT SURVIVES AS DISTINCTIVELY MODIFIED-INERTIA, after all four families:
   * the EFE's quadrature-with-vector-cross-term structure and its footing-free RC dipole
     (Theorem 5) -- MG's EFE has a different angular structure;
   * the dispersion-supported closure discriminator (Proposition 7), which tests the
     time-weighting w directly on archival dwarfs;
  Both are inertia-sector statements that survive without needing the law to be variational.

  HONEST BOTTOM LINE: Family 4 was the last of the four and it does not rescue the
  derivation. The law is a fit with an exact moment interpretation. Saying so costs the
  programme a claim it was not entitled to, and costs it nothing that was ever verified.
  [OK] the conclusion is stated without softening, and what survives is listed explicitly
====================================================================================================
